# Overview
#
# A contrastive `while` sets one claim against another (`reads are fast,
# while the writes wait for the lock`). `while` also marks time (`while
# the lock is held`), so a reader who meets it after a comma must decide
# which of the two it marks. The fix writes `but` for a contrast or
# splits the sentence. The rule sits at Recommended, because a `while`
# after a comma can also mark time.
#
# Expression
#
# The pattern matches `while` after a comma and before a determiner, a
# pronoun, a proper noun, or a noun (`reads are fast, while requests are
# slow`). A determiner is a word that introduces a noun, such as `the`,
# `a`, or `this`. A proper noun is a name that takes a capital letter,
# such as `Kubernetes`. Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word
# with a tag for its word class. The pattern names these classes as
# `DET` (a determiner), `PRON` (a pronoun), `PROPN` (a proper noun), and
# `NOUN` (a noun).
#
# The tagger tags some `-ing` forms of a verb as nouns (`building`), and
# a `while` before one marks time (`, while building the image`). `!PROG`
# drops the `-ing` form of a verb from the noun branch, so that `while`
# passes. The guard also passes a contrast that opens with an `-ing`
# word (`, while binding cycles may run concurrently`).
#
# The tagger tags some nouns as verbs, because each is also a verb form
# (`writes`, `runs`). `verb` holds a word with the tag `VERB` (a verb)
# or `AUX` (a helping verb). The `verb` branch matches two such words in
# a row (`reads are fast, while writes are slow`). It reads the first
# word as the subject of the second, and so as a noun. `!PROG` drops
# the `-ing` form from `verb` too, because an `-ing` form and the verb
# on either side of it are one verb with no subject (`, while being
# written`, `, while left running`).
#
# Every other `while` passes, even when it marks a contrast (`reads are
# fast, while large writes are slow`).
#
# By default, a Weir match stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"`
# lets the pattern cross one.
#
# False Positives
#
# `while` with no comma before it passes, because the rule takes the
# comma as the sign of a contrast. After a comma, `while` before a verb
# with no subject passes too, because it marks time there (`, while
# connected to the server`, `, while holding the lock`, `, while
# building the image`).
#
# A `while` after a comma that marks time still draws a finding when a
# subject follows it (`It is served anyway, while the refresher catches
# up`). That sentence reads one way after the comma goes. `PROG` does
# not match every `-ing` word, so `, while encoding` still draws a
# finding. The `verb` branch also reports a verb of two words that has
# no subject (`, while let go by the scheduler`).
#
# The noun branch also reports `while` as the name of a loop in a list
# of keywords (`for loops, while loops, and until loops`). A contrast
# can open with the same words (`, while loops with no exit spin
# forever`), so the branch has no guard for `loop` or `statement`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 59 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits marked a contrast. All 9 hits before a word with the `NOUN` tag
# marked a contrast, and so did the one hit of the `verb` branch
# (`while Ignore will cause`).
expr verb <[VERB, AUX], !PROG>
expr main , (while [DET, PRON, PROPN, <NOUN, !PROG>, (@verb @verb)])
let message "Contrastive `while`. Write `but` for a contrast, or split the sentence."
let description "Flags `while` after a comma, where it usually marks a contrast that `but` marks in one reading."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The hook waits while the lock is held."
